Hello to the Vuly team. 

I have long wanted to work as a Product Designer in a tangible and physical environment. The aspect of play and exploration that comes from interacting with a physical design is unlike anything else we can experience. Humans desire experience that is tied to our senses that give us the feedback and reaction so we can understand our world better. This is no more important in our developing and younger years when play and experience is important for how we can navigate our world better.

I have 10+ years experience as a  experiential designer working with stakeholders, customers, and other involved parties to make sure work and outcomes are delivered. I have translated concepts into briefs, pitches, milestones, that have delivered outcomes of end-to-end customer journey experiences that have considered the marketing, customer product interest, selection & ordering, onboarding, lifecycle, and potential off-boarding. I believe that the customer is golden and should be treated as such along every step of the way. 

In a physical product, every product has a story and fits a need. Being able to help customers and users navigate its potential is imperative to help reduce pain points, instructions, and support. This is why I feel testing, interviews, and prototyping are so important in product design. These steps help companies create products that customers want to buy, and make users return to them. Steps like these can help shape and drive direction for future opportunities in a business, their products, and their features. 

As a designer who works closely with product teams, and needs to have influence in decision making, I practice balancing the desirability, feasibility, viability, and usability of my work. When opportunities get presented or uncovered, they are validated to help make sure they can find a practice within here. When some opportunities are delivered, they may open up others that did not find the right practice. 

I am passionate about tangible design. I love interacting with physical objects to help learn and understand interfaces and environments better. This comes in design practice as well, sketching, drawing, whiteboards, post-its. It all feels more complete. I am a woodworking hobbyist that likes to think of solutions and concepts around “what function will this serve” when completed. 

I would love to have the opportunity to share my passion for physical design with Vuly and help with creating and bringing to market products that will engage their customers and bring joy to them .

